The reduced knee and loaded-cup demonstrations each implement six connections. On each side, Ia excites its own MN and inhibits the antagonist MN, and Ib inhibits its own MN. The Ia block combines a position signal and signed velocity and clips its output to \qtyrange{0}{10}{
ano\ampere}. For knee angle $q$ and angular range $R$, the extensor velocity input is $\dot q/R$. The implemented flexor path applies a second gain of $-1/R$ to that signal, giving $-\dot q/R^2$. In the cup model, the two muscles receive opposite signed deviations and velocities, each divided by \qty{0.3}{adian}. Both models feed tensile force directly to Ib. The knee's extensor and flexor gains are 0.0200 and \qty{0.0222}{
ano\ampere\per
ewton}, respectively. Both cup gains are \qty{0.008}{
ano\ampere\per
ewton}. The knee uses a simplified linear actuator. In the cup, filtered activation $A$ supplies $620A$\,kPa to each characterized BPA20 block. Neither circuit has an independent group II or foot-contact input.

I ran a control and a stimulated trial continuously for \qty{50}{\second} from the same initial knee flexion of \qty{0.26}{adian}, zero angular velocity and activation, and MN voltages of \qty{-52}{\milli\volt}. Tonic drive was \qty{4.2}{
ano\ampere} to the extensor MN and \qty{2.5}{
ano\ampere} to the flexor MN. In the stimulated trial I added \qty{2}{
ano\ampere} at \path{SN_Ia_ext}, after the spindle transducer and before the extensor Ia sensory-neuron membrane, for $30.000\leq t<30.030$\,s. Angle, speed, six membrane states, and two activations settled before stimulation. I report the negative of the model's internal flexion coordinate so that positive excursions indicate extension. The baseline was the mean control angle for $25\leq t<30$\,s, \ang{-42.6392}. The initial extension came from increased extensor force, which rose from \qty{7.37}{
ewton} at pulse onset to \qty{25.94}{
ewton} at pulse offset. The flexor MN output was zero at baseline, and flexor force was negligible during the pulse. Flexor force subsequently exceeded \qty{0.001}{
ewton} at \qty{38}{\milli\second} after onset and reached \qty{14.4}{
ewton} before maximum knee extension.

With ground contact disabled, both feet recorded zero force. The stage-2 suspension spring supplied a mean \qty{768.662}{
ewton} over $5\leq t<16$\,s. The interval-start samples end at \qty{15.998}{\second}. Mean support was \qty{99.860}{\percent} of the original denominator, \qty{769.737726}{
ewton} from \qty{78.4646}{\kilogram}, which includes a fixed \qty{0.1}{\kilogram} ground body. Dividing the same mean force by the moving pelvis-subtree weight, \qty{768.756726}{
ewton} from \qty{78.3646}{\kilogram}, gives \qty{99.9877}{\percent}. Figure~ef{fig:sns_biw2l_air} shows force in newtons with the aligned states, which use interval-end timestamps. This apparatus isolates suspended limb motion and retains frontal guidance. Higher PF-to-MN expression triggered the predictive knee-extension stop. The reported configuration completed the air stages with that guard and without clipping joint state. Standing and ground stepping remain unverified for this controller.

The input vector has four heel/toe contact ports, twelve Ib ports, two brief initiation inputs, and four tonic RG drives. Each contact port reached \qty{5.30489}{
ano\ampere} in the intact trial. Each leg's pooled extensor-force current went to all six Ib ports on that side, with maxima of \qty{0.12237}{
ano\ampere} left and \qty{0.12263}{
ano\ampere} right. The circuit has internal Ia- and II-labeled relay connections, but this evaluator supplies neither independent per-muscle length nor velocity through an external Ia or II port.

The model completed \qty{28}{\second}. During $16\leq t<28$\,s, mean vertical harness force was \qty{570.20625}{
ewton}, or \qty{77.5}{\percent} of the \qty{735.75}{
ewton} weight of the \qty{75}{\kilogram} model. Seven right and six left unloaded, whole-foot-clear intervals gave twelve alternations. Each interval required foot-mesh clearance above \qty{5}{\milli\meter}, ipsilateral normal load below \qty{7.36}{
ewton}, contralateral load above \qty{15}{
ewton}, and duration of at least \qty{80}{\milli\second}. The \qty{2}{\milli\second} guard calculation recorded whole-trial minimum anatomical flexion of \ang{11.20} right and \ang{5.10} left against a \ang{1} limit. Figure~ef{fig:app_cad_supported_traces} shows the signed native angle sampled every \qty{10}{\milli\second}, which can miss a physics-step extremum. Whole-trial world-forward displacement was \qty{-74.7}{\milli\meter} with orientation and lateral guidance active.
